\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is the full antisymmetric continuum electronic Coulomb ground-energy promise problem QMA-hard when every nucleus has charge one, distinct positions are rational, electron count is unary, and rational thresholds are separated by at least inverse input length? Require deterministic polynomial-time reductions producing polynomially many nuclei and electrons, polynomial bit lengths, and gap at least one.

